Key assertions:
- `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex`: expects `identityKey` to match `[0-9a-f]{64}` (lower-case SHA-256 hex). The current `IdentityKey.forOwner` returns a plain `"+61...|email|HOUSEHOLDID"` string, so this assertion would fail against the present implementation.
- `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity`: creates an owner, then re-posts an identical copy (same telephone/email/lastName/postcode) expecting **409 Conflict**. This passes under the current household-id + telephone block in `EnsureUniqueIdentity`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the shared harness (322 lines). Relevant helpers:
- `structuredOwner()` (lines 136-142): unique last name, `addressLine1`, `postcode "2000"`.
- `createOwner`/`createOwnerOk`/`createOwnerWithKey` (155-168), `fetchOwner` (200-204), `deleteOwner` (soft-delete note, 193-197).
- `sha256hex` (282-294) and `shaHex` (297-299): full lower-case SHA-256 hex and first-n upper-case hex — the test's own oracle for hashing.
- `uniqueTelephone()` (102-104) returns `04XXXXXXXX`, matching the `+61` 9-national-digit rule.

These are the only two test files touching identity/household/duplicate/telephone/blocklist behavior; there are no separate unit tests for `IdentityKey`, `HouseholdNormalizer`, `TelephoneNormalizer`, or `DisposableEmailDomains`.

